--- check
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => t.$$("#root .card").length >= 12, 2000), "The cards have gone. Look for a red error in the Console: it names the line.");
const input = t.$("#root input");
t.expect(input, "I can't find the search box. Add an `<input />` inside `<div className=\"search\">`, in the hero section.");
t.expect(input.closest(".search"), "Put the input inside `<div className=\"search\">`, so it gets the search box style.");
const n = () => t.$$("#root .card").length;
await t.type(t.$("#root input"), "office");
if (!(await t.waitFor(() => n() === 1, 1500))) {
  t.expect(!t.errors.some((e) => /toLowerCase is not a function/.test(e)), "When I typed, the page crashed: `search` holds the whole event instead of the text. Use `onChange={(e) => setSearch(e.target.value)}`.");
  t.expect(t.$("#root input").value === "office", "When I typed in the box, the text disappeared. Add `onChange={(e) => setSearch(e.target.value)}` to the input.");
  t.expect(n() !== 12, "I typed \"office\", but all 12 cards are still there. Filter the shows with the search text, and `map` the filtered list instead of `shows`.");
  t.fail(`I typed "office" and got ${n()} cards. Only The Office should be left. Check the filter: \`show.name.toLowerCase().includes(search.toLowerCase())\`.`);
}
t.expect(t.text("#root .card h3") === "The Office", `I typed "office" and got the card "${t.text("#root .card h3")}". It should be The Office.`);
await t.type(t.$("#root input"), "Dark");
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => n() === 1 && t.text("#root .card h3") === "Dark", 1500), "I typed \"Dark\" with a capital D and didn't get the show Dark. Make the search ignore capitals: use `toLowerCase()` on both the name and the search text.");
await t.type(t.$("#root input"), "zzz");
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => n() === 0, 1500), `I typed "zzz" and ${n()} cards stayed. No show matches, so the grid should be empty.`);
await t.type(t.$("#root input"), "");
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => n() === 12, 1500), "When the box is empty again, all 12 shows should come back.");
--- learned
Type, the state changes, React redraws the filtered list. The ShowShelf React app you saw this morning is built from exactly these pieces: components, props, state and `map`.
--- summary
A controlled input: `value={state}` and an `onChange` that sets the state; filter the data and map the result.
--- mistakes
- `value={search}` without `onChange`: the box can't be typed in.
- Still mapping `shows` instead of the filtered list, so nothing changes.
- Using `onChange={setSearch}`: that saves the whole event, not the text. Use `(e) => setSearch(e.target.value)`.
--- teacher
If a student asks why the My List ticks disappear when a card is filtered out and comes back: the card's state goes away with the card. In the full app, the list lives in `App` and is passed down as a prop. That's a good question to end the React part on.


=== step server-hello
title: Your first server route
minutes: 15
slot: server
template: server
pagename: mylist.html
--- explain
Until now, all your code ran in the **browser**, on the visitor's computer. A **server** is a program on another computer that waits for **requests** and sends back **responses**. When your page called `fetch("https://api.tvmaze.com/...")`, TVmaze's server answered. Now you write the server side.

**Express** is a popular way to write a server in JavaScript, with Node.js. You describe **routes**: "when someone asks for *this address* with *this method*, run *this function*":

```js
app.get("/api/time", (req, res) => {
  res.json({ time: "half past four" });
});
```

- `app.get` handles `GET` requests (asking for something) to the address `/api/time`.
- `req`, the **request**, holds what the visitor sent. `res`, the **response**, is how you answer.
- `res.json(...)` sends a JavaScript object back as JSON.

> In a real project, server.js runs with Node.js on a server, and the page lives somewhere else. In the Lab, a pretend Express runs inside the preview: when the page calls `fetch("/api/...")`, the Lab hands the request to your code instead of sending it over the internet. The page (the **mylist.html** tab) is fixed: you only write the server.
--- task
Under `// Your routes go here`, add a route for `GET /api/hello` that answers with an object like `{ message: "Hello from the server!" }`.

When it works, the top of the page shows what your server said. Then try the **Try a request** box at the bottom of the preview: press **Send**.
--- hint
Follow the example: `app.get("/api/hello", (req, res) => { ... });`. Inside the curly brackets, answer with `res.json({ message: "..." })`.
--- hint
```js
app.get("/api/hello", (req, res) => {
  res.json({ message: "Hello from the server!" });
});
```
--- solution-replace
// Your routes go here


app.listen(3000, () => {
=>
// Your routes go here

app.get("/api/hello", (req, res) => {
  res.json({ message: "Hello from the server!" });
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
--- check
const call = async (method, path, body) => {
  const opts = { method, headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" } };
  if (body !== undefined) opts.body = JSON.stringify(body);
  let r;
  try { r = await t.win.fetch(path, opts); } catch (e) { t.fail("The page can't reach your server. Look for a red error in the Console: server.js must run without errors, and it needs the line `const app = express();`."); }
  const text = await r.text();
  let data = text;
  try { data = text ? JSON.parse(text) : null; } catch (e) { /* plain text */ }
  return { status: r.status, data, error: data && typeof data === "object" && typeof data.error === "string" ? data.error : "" };
};
const r = await call("GET", "/api/hello");
t.expect(r.status !== 404, "There's no route for `GET /api/hello` yet (the server answered 404 Not Found). Add `app.get(\"/api/hello\", (req, res) => { ... });` under the comment. Check the address: it starts with a `/`.");
t.expect(r.status !== 504, "Your route never answered. Inside it, send the answer: `res.json({ message: \"Hello from the server!\" });`.");
t.expect(r.status !== 500, "Your route crashed (500). Read the red error in the Console.");
t.expect(r.status === 200, `The server answered ${r.status}. A normal answer is 200: use \`res.json(...)\` without a status.`);
t.expect(r.data && typeof r.data === "object" && !Array.isArray(r.data), `Your server answered ${JSON.stringify(r.data)}. Answer with an object, using \`res.json\`: \`res.json({ message: "Hello from the server!" })\`.`);
t.expect(typeof r.data.message === "string" && r.data.message.trim(), `Your server answered ${JSON.stringify(r.data)}. Put the text in a property called \`message\`: \`{ message: "Hello from the server!" }\`.`);
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => /Server says/.test(t.text("#hello")), 2000), "Your route works, but the page didn't show it. Press **Run again** above the preview, then check again.");
--- learned
You wrote a **route**: a method (`GET`), an address (`/api/hello`) and a function that answers. That's the heart of every Express server. The line `GET /api/hello → 200` in the Console means "OK".
--- summary
A route: `app.get("/path", (req, res) => { res.json(...); })`.
--- mistakes
- `res.send("Hello")` instead of `res.json({ message: ... })`: the answer is plain text, not an object.
- An address without the first `/`, or a typo like `/api/helo`.
- Putting the route inside the `{ }` of `app.listen(...)`. It works, but routes belong above it.
--- teacher
The pretend server is only a stand-in: it understands the same code as Express for today's features. If students ask where Node.js is: it's what the teacher used this morning in the terminal (`npm run dev`).


=== step server-list
title: Send My List
minutes: 10
slot: server
template: server
pagename: mylist.html
--- explain
A server usually keeps data and sends it to whoever asks. For now, the data lives in a variable in the server's memory:

```js
let playlist = [
  { id: 1, title: "Yesterday" },
  { id: 2, title: "Help!" },
];

app.get("/api/playlist", (req, res) => {
  res.json(playlist);
});
```

`res.json` can send any value: an object, or here, an array of objects. The page receives exactly this array from its `fetch`.

The addresses start with `/api/` by habit. An **API** (*Application Programming Interface*) is a set of addresses for programs, not for people: the page's JavaScript reads them, not the visitor.

We use `let`, not `const`, because in step 11 you'll replace the whole list with a new one.
--- task
1. Above `app.listen`, create `let myList` with at least two shows. Each show is an object with a number `id` and a `name`, like `{ id: 1, name: "Friends" }`.
