t.expect(!(r.status === 404 && /^Cannot |not for /.test(r.error)), "There's no route for `DELETE /api/mylist/:id` yet. Add `app.delete(\"/api/mylist/:id\", (req, res) => { ... });`.");
t.expect(r.status !== 404, `\`DELETE /api/mylist/${first.id}\` answered 404, but show ${first.id} (${first.name}) is in the list. Everything in an address is text: compare with \`Number(req.params.id)\`.`);
t.expect(r.status !== 504, "Your DELETE route never answered. When the show is removed, answer `res.status(204).end();`.");
t.expect(r.status !== 500, "Your DELETE route crashed (500). Read the red error in the Console. (If it says \"Assignment to constant variable\", create `myList` with `let`.)");
t.expect(r.status === 204, `Removing a show answered ${r.status}. When it worked, answer \`res.status(204).end();\`.`);
const after = await call("GET", "/api/mylist");
t.expect(!after.data.some((s) => s.id === first.id), "The server answered 204, but the show is still in the list. Remove it: `myList = myList.filter((show) => show.id !== id);`.");
t.expect(after.data.length === list.data.length - 1, "Only the show with that id should go. Check the filter: `show.id !== id`.");
const nf = await call("DELETE", "/api/mylist/424242");
t.expect(nf.status !== 204 && nf.status !== 200, "I removed id 424242, which isn't in the list, and the server said it worked. First look for the show with `find`, and answer 404 when there's none.");
t.expect(nf.status === 404, `Removing a show that isn't in the list (id 424242) answered ${nf.status}. It should answer 404.`);
t.expect(nf.error.trim(), "Send a message with the 404: `res.status(404).json({ error: \"That show is not in My List\" })`.");
t.expect(pageOk, "Your route works, but the page's Remove button didn't remove a show. Press **Run again** and try it yourself.");
--- learned
A route parameter (`:id`) puts a value in the address, and `req.params` reads it. The status codes tell the page exactly what happened: 204 removed, 404 not found.
--- summary
`app.delete("/api/mylist/:id", ...)`: `Number(req.params.id)`, 404 when it's missing, 204 when it's done.
--- mistakes
- Forgetting `Number(...)`: `"2" === 2` is false, so every show is "not found".
- `myList.filter(...)` without `myList =` in front: the filter makes a new list, but nobody keeps it.
- `const myList`: then `myList = ...` crashes with "Assignment to constant variable".


=== step server-log
title: Read the request log
minutes: 10
slot: server
template: server
pagename: mylist.html
console: open
--- explain
Look at the **Console** under the preview. Every request to your server leaves a line like `GET /api/mylist → 200`: the method, the address, and the **status code** your server answered. Developers read logs like this all day to see what's going on.

The status codes you've met today:

- `200` **OK**: here's what you asked for
- `201` **Created**: I saved your new thing
- `204` **No Content**: done, nothing to send back
- `400` **Bad Request**: your data is wrong
- `404` **Not Found**: no such thing, or no such route
- `500` **Internal Server Error**: the server crashed

The first digit tells you at once who has a problem: **2** means success, **4** means the request was wrong, **5** means the server has a problem.

Your own `console.log` in a route appears in the same place. A real server prints its messages in the terminal where it runs, like the teacher's laptop this morning.
--- task
1. In the preview, add a show, add an empty name, remove a show, and send `GET /api/nothing` with **Try a request**. Watch which codes appear in the Console.
2. In your POST route, right before the answer, add a `console.log` that prints the name of the new show, like `console.log("Added:", item.name);`. Add a show again, and find your message in the Console.
--- hint
The `console.log` goes inside `app.post(...)`, after `myList.push(item);` and before `res.status(201)...`. A line after the answer would work too, but it's clearer to log first.
--- hint
```js
  myList.push(item);
  console.log("Added:", item.name);
  res.status(201).json(item);
```
--- solution-replace
  myList.push(item);
=>
  myList.push(item);
  console.log("Added:", item.name);
--- check
const call = async (method, path, body) => {
  const opts = { method, headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" } };
  if (body !== undefined) opts.body = JSON.stringify(body);
  let r;
  try { r = await t.win.fetch(path, opts); } catch (e) { t.fail("The page can't reach your server. Look for a red error in the Console: server.js must run without errors, and it needs the line `const app = express();`."); }
  const text = await r.text();
  let data = text;
  try { data = text ? JSON.parse(text) : null; } catch (e) { /* plain text */ }
  return { status: r.status, data, error: data && typeof data === "object" && typeof data.error === "string" ? data.error : "" };
};
const r = await call("POST", "/api/mylist", { name: "Band of Brothers" });
t.expect(r.status === 201, `Adding a show should still answer 201, but it answered ${r.status}. Keep your POST route from the earlier steps working.`);
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => t.logged("Band of Brothers"), 1000), "I added the show Band of Brothers, but your server didn't print its name. In the POST route, before `res.status(201)...`, add `console.log(\"Added:\", item.name);` (with the name of your own variable).");
--- learned
Logs show what a server did, request by request. The first digit of a status code tells you who has a problem: 2 all fine, 4 the request, 5 the server.
--- summary
Status codes: 2xx success, 4xx a wrong request, 5xx a server problem; a server's `console.log` goes to its log.
--- mistakes
- Putting the `console.log` in the GET route instead of POST.
- `console.log("item.name")` in quotes: it prints the words, not the show's name.
--- teacher
A good discussion to end the day: which part of the ShowShelf you know is the page, and which part could be a server? (The page draws; TVmaze and My List are data a server could keep.) If there's time, let students break a route on purpose (`throw new Error("Oops")`) to see a 500.


=== step tvmaze-effect
title: Shows from TVmaze with useEffect
minutes: 20
slot: app
template: react
bonus: yes
--- explain
Your cards come from the fixed `shows` list. Let's load shows from TVmaze with `fetch` instead.

A component's function runs again every time React draws it, so a `fetch` written straight in `App` would run again and again. **`useEffect`** runs code *after* React has drawn, and with an empty array `[]` at the end, only **once**:

```jsx
const [songs, setSongs] = React.useState([]);

React.useEffect(() => {
  async function load() {
    const response = await fetch("https://example.com/songs");
    const data = await response.json();
    setSongs(data);
  }
  load();
}, []);
```

The page first draws with an empty list. When the answer arrives, `setSongs` stores it, and React draws again.

TVmaze's shows look a little different from ours (for example, the rating is in `rating.average`), so we've added a `toShow` function at the bottom that turns one TVmaze show into our shape.
--- task
In `App`:

1. Create state `tvShows` that starts as an empty array: `React.useState([])`.
2. Add a `React.useEffect` that fetches `https://api.tvmaze.com/shows?page=0`, turns every show into our shape with `data.map(toShow)`, and stores the result with `setTvShows`.
3. Filter `tvShows` instead of `shows`.

Without internet, the Lab answers with 12 saved sample shows, so the cards look the same as before, but now they come from `fetch`.
--- starter-append
// TVmaze's shows look different from ours. This turns one of them into our shape.
function toShow(tv) {
  return {
    id: tv.id,
    name: tv.name,
    genres: tv.genres,
    rating: tv.rating.average,
  };
}
